{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794847601301,"sessionID":"ses_e1abf3e72ffecbrpVf6MWZonWP","part":{"partID":"prt_1e540ef1c002Ufn2xpH7TFhMEw","sessionID":"ses_e1abf3e72ffecbrpVf6MWZonWP","messageID":"msg_1e540c5d3001xEGsTW9qm6NyRC","type":"tool","id":"call_303b89e1433043799bc2525b","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/SKILL.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/SKILL.md, lines 1-355\n1: ---\n2: name: deep-review\n3: description: \"Autonomous iterative code-review loop with externalized state, convergence detection, P0/P1/P2 findings, fresh context per pass.\"\n4: argument-hint: \"[target] [:auto|:confirm] [--max-iterations=N] [--convergence=N] [--stop-policy=convergence|max-iterations]\"\n5: version: 1.11.17.0\n6: allowed-tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob, Task]\n7: ---\n8: <!-- Note: Task is for the command executor (loop management); @deep-review agent is LEAF-only (no Task). No WebFetch: review is code-only. -->\n9: \n10: # Autonomous Deep Review Loop\n11: \n12: Iterative code review and quality auditing protocol with fresh context per iteration, externalized state, convergence detection, and severity-weighted findings (P0/P1/P2).\n13: \n14: Runtime path resolution:\n15: - OpenCode runtime: `.opencode/agents/*.md`, a link to the `.skilled/agents/*.md` source\n16: - Claude runtime: `.claude/agents/*.md`\n17: - Other runtimes: see `.skilled/bin/agent-mirror-crosswalk.md`\n18: \n19: Convergence threshold semantics and sibling-parity notes (deep-review 0.10 vs deep-research 0.05) live in `references/convergence/convergence.md` §1 under \"Threshold Semantics and Sibling Parity\".\n20: \n21: ## 1. WHEN TO USE\n22: \n23: ### When to Use This Skill\n24: \n25: Use this skill when:\n26: - Code quality audit requiring multiple rounds across different review dimensions\n27: - Spec folder validation requiring cross-reference checks between docs and implementation\n28: - Release readiness check before shipping a feature or component\n29: - Finding misalignments between spec documents and actual code\n30: - Verifying cross-references across documentation, agents, commands, and code\n31: - Iterative review where each dimension's findings inform subsequent dimensions\n32: - Unattended or overnight audit sessions\n33: \n34: ### When NOT to Use\n35: \n36: - Simple single-pass code review (use `sk-code`'s code-review mode instead)\n37: - Known issues that just need fixing (go directly to implementation)\n38: - Implementation tasks (use `sk-code` or `/speckit:implement`)\n39: - Quick one-file checks (use direct Grep/Read)\n40: - Fewer than 2 review dimensions needed (single-pass suffices)\n41: \n42: ### FORBIDDEN INVOCATION PATTERNS\n43: \n44: This skill is invoked EXCLUSIVELY through the `/deep:review` command. The command's YAML workflow owns state, dispatch, and convergence.\n45: \n46: **NEVER:**\n47: - Write a custom bash/shell dispatcher to parallelize iterations (ad-hoc shell fan-out)\n48: - Invoke any CLI executor (cli-codex, cli-claude-code, cli-opencode, cli-cursor, cli-devin, cli-pi, cli-deepseek-harness) directly in a loop to simulate iterations\n49: - Manually write iteration prompts to `/tmp` and dispatch them via `copilot -p`\n50: - Dispatch the `@deep-review` LEAF agent via the Task tool for iteration loops (the agent is LEAF, a single iteration, and MUST be driven by the command's workflow)\n51: - Skip the state machine: `deep-review-state.jsonl`, `deep-review-config.json`, `deltas/`, `prompts/`, `logs/`\n52: - Manage iteration state outside the resolved local review packet under `{spec_folder}/review/`\n53: \n54: **COMMAND-DRIVEN FAN-OUT IS SUPPORTED:** use `--executor`/`--executors`/`--concurrency` flags on `/deep:review`. The command's YAML `step_fanout_spawn` owns multi-lineage dispatch; `fanout-merge.cjs` applies strongest-restriction (any lineage with an active P0, or a required quality gate still failing at its stop → merged FAIL). This is not ad-hoc shell dispatch — it is the canonical fan-out path. Intra-lineage wave orchestration remains deferred.\n55: \n56: **ALWAYS:**\n57: - Invoke via `/deep:review :auto` or `/deep:review :confirm`\n58: - Let the command's YAML workflow own dispatch (auto: `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review.yaml`)\n59: - Record each leaf iteration through the append gateway (`runtime/scripts/append-mode-event.cjs --mode review --run-directory <spec folder> --event-json <file>`) — the gateway is the SINGLE state-log writer: it authorizes, fences, and receipts the write, then refreshes `{state_paths.state_log}` from the ledger\n60: - Treat `runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs` as the reducer for DERIVED artifacts only (finding registry, dashboard metrics, strategy updates); it never writes the state log\n61: - Require every iteration to produce BOTH the markdown narrative AND the JSONL delta (dispatch scripts must fail if either is missing)\n62: - Use `resolveArtifactRoot(specFolder, 'review')` from `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/shared/review-research-paths.cjs` to locate the canonical review root\n63: \n64: ### Trigger Phrases\n65: \n66: - \"review code quality\" / \"audit this code\"\n67: - \"audit spec folder\" / \"validate spec completeness\"\n68: - \"release readiness check\" / \"pre-release review\"\n69: - \"find misalignments\" (between spec and implementation)\n70: - \"verify cross-references\" (across docs and code)\n71: - \"deep review\" / \"iterative review\" / \"review loop\"\n72: - \"quality audit\" / \"convergence detection\"\n73: \n74: ### Keyword Triggers\n75: \n76: `deep review`, `convergence review`, `iterative review`, `review loop`, `release readiness`, `spec folder review`, `convergence detection`, `quality audit`, `find misalignments`, `verify cross-references`, `pre-release review`, `audit spec folder`\n77: \n78: ---\n79: \n80: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n81: \n82: The `system-deep-loop` hub routes an iterative review loop here, and its `review` mode signal selects this packet.\n83: The signal fires when a request names a deep review, a convergence review, a review loop, a release readiness check or a spec-folder audit.\n84: The loop runs only through `/deep:review`, whose workflow owns state, dispatch and convergence.\n85: \n86: `references/protocol/quick-reference.md` loads first on every invocation.\n87: The review phase named by the dispatch context then selects the loop protocol, the convergence documents or the state documents.\n88: \n89: The routing pseudocode in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) is the one route from a request to a resource set.\n90: A request whose intent scores stay under the threshold returns `UNKNOWN_FALLBACK` with a disambiguation checklist, never a silent default.\n91: Read [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n92: \n93: ---\n94: \n95: ## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n96: \n97: ### Architecture\n98: \n99: `/deep:review` owns the loop. The YAML workflow initializes state, dispatches one LEAF review iteration at a time, evaluates convergence, synthesizes `review-report.md`, and saves continuity. The LEAF agent reads state, reviews one dimension, writes `iteration-NNN.md`, updates strategy, and appends JSONL. Full 4-phase lifecycle: `references/protocol/loop-protocol.md`.\n100: \n101: ### Resource Map Coverage Gate\n102: \n103: When `{spec_folder}/resource-map.md` exists at init, deep review treats it as a mandatory audit input and does four things:\n104: \n105: - Persists `resource_map_present` in config.\n106: - Seeds `Known Context` with a snapshot of the map.\n107: - Has at least one iteration audit `target_files` coverage against the map: touched, not-touched with `expected-by-scope`-versus-`gap`, and absent-from-map, with findings tagged `resource-map-coverage`.\n108: - Has synthesis add the `## Resource Map Coverage Gate` report section and a converged `resource-map.md`. Disable this with `--no-resource-map`.\n109: \n110: Absent at init, the gate and section are skipped without failing the loop.\n111: Full sequencing: `references/protocol/loop-protocol.md` (init 9a, iteration 3b, synthesis 4). Schema: `references/state/state-format.md`.\n112: \n113: ### Bounded Context Snapshot Replacement\n114: \n115: During initialization, capture a bounded, pointer-based context snapshot in `deep-review-strategy.md` `Known Context` before the first dimension runs.\n116: The snapshot holds:\n117: \n118: - Target pointers: files, specs, symbols and resource-map entries.\n119: - The claimed behavior and acceptance criteria to verify.\n120: - Reuse and convention pointers.\n121: - Risk areas, missing context, stale-graph caveats and out-of-scope areas.\n122: \n123: Do not create a context-loop report or widen scope beyond the declared target.\n124: Use `@context` for quick retrieval, and `/speckit:plan` only when findings require implementation planning.\n125: \n126: ### State Packet Location\n127: \n128: The review state packet always lives under the target spec's local `review/` folder.\n129: Root-spec targets use `{spec_folder}/review/` directly.\n130: Child-phase and sub-phase targets use **flat-first** placement: the first run writes flat, and a `pt-NN` sibling packet is allocated only when prior content already exists for a different target.\n131: Full resolution rule and worked examples: `references/state/state-format.md` §1.\n132: \n133: Core artifacts: `deep-review-config.json`, `deep-review-state.jsonl`, `deep-review-strategy.md`, `deep-review-dashboard.md`, `.deep-review-pause`, `resource-map.md`, `review-report.md`, and `iterations/iteration-NNN.md`.\n134: \n135: The loop writes `review/review-report.md` itself at synthesis rather than scaffolding it through `create.sh --level review`.\n136: The report is assembled from the findings registry and the JSONL state, so its content exists only once the loop has run, and a scaffolded skeleton would be overwritten whole.\n137: `create.sh --level review` exists for a review packet authored by hand, and its template carries the same nine sections the loop emits.\n138: \n139: ### Core Innovation: Fresh Context Per Iteration\n140: \n141: Each agent dispatch gets a fresh context window; state continuity comes from files, not memory, preventing accumulated findings from biasing later dimensions. Init writes config/strategy/JSONL; each loop reads state, checks convergence, dispatches one dimension, records findings, and reduces state; synthesis compiles the report and saves continuity.\n142: \n143: ### Review Dimensions\n144: \n145: The four primary review dimensions (configured in `assets/review-mode-contract.yaml`):\n146: \n147: | Dimension | Focus | Key Questions |\n148: |-----------|-------|---------------|\n149: | **Correctness** | Logic, behavior, error handling | Behavior matches claims? Edge cases handled? |\n150: | **Security** | Vulnerabilities, exposure, trust boundaries | Inputs validated? Credentials exposed? |\n151: | **Spec-Alignment / Traceability** | Spec vs. implementation fidelity | Code matches spec.md? Planned items present? |\n152: | **Completeness / Maintainability** | Coverage, dead code, documentation | TODOs resolved? Code self-documenting? |\n153: \n154: ### Lifecycle + Reducer Contract\n155: \n156: Review mode is lineage-aware.\n157: \n158: - **Lifecycle modes:** `new`, `resume`, and `restart`.\n159: - **Required lineage fields:** `sessionId`, `parentSessionId`, `lineageMode`, `generation`, `continuedFromRun`, and `releaseReadinessState`.\n160: - **Reducer contract:** consumes the latest JSONL delta, the new iteration file, and prior reduced state, then emits finding registry, dashboard metrics, and strategy updates.\n161: \n162: ### Severity Classification\n163: \n164: | Severity | Criteria | Blocking |\n165: |----------|----------|---------|\n166: | **P0** | Correctness failure, security vulnerability, spec contradiction | Yes, blocks PASS verdict |\n167: | **P1** | Degraded behavior, incomplete implementation, missing validation | Conditional, triggers CONDITIONAL verdict |\n168: | **P2** | Style, naming, minor improvements, documentation gaps | No, PASS with advisories |\n169: \n170: P0 and P1 also follow the reach calibration in `sk-code-review`'s `references/review-core.md` §2.\n171: A blocking finding names an input or call path the code's stated contract allows, or a real caller that sends the failing input.\n172: \"Missing validation\" is P1 only when the unvalidated input is in reach. Validation the code never claimed, for input outside its stated contract, is P2 hardening.\n173: At a trust boundary every value the outside can send is in reach.\n174: \n175: The scale has exactly three tiers, and no fourth is reserved.\n176: A severity outside P0/P1/P2 is not a fourth tier. That covers a `P3` inherited from an older four-level convention, and any unlisted word.\n177: Collapse it to the tier its impact actually matches (a `P3` becomes `P2`) and keep the original rating visible in the finding, so the rating is converted rather than lost.\n178: An unconverted out-of-scale severity reaches the cross-lineage merge unranked, where it sorts below every P2 and cannot raise the merged verdict.\n179: \n180: ### Verdicts\n181: \n182: | Verdict | Condition |\n183: |---------|-----------|\n184: | **PASS** | No P0/P1 findings and no open search debt, P2 findings recorded as advisories (`hasAdvisories: true`) |\n185: | **CONDITIONAL** | P1 findings present, or open search debt remains, with remediation plan included in report |\n186: | **FAIL** | Any P0 finding confirmed after adversarial self-check, or a required quality gate (evidence, scope, coverage) still failing at the terminal stop |\n187: \n188: ### Acceptance-Coverage Signal\n189: \n190: When the review target is a spec folder, deep review reflects the `AC_COVERAGE` validation signal in synthesis for Level 2+ folders.\n191: The signal applies only once `checklist.md` exists and `implementation-summary.md` is in-progress or later. Level 1 folders and fresh scaffolds are exempt.\n192: It is advisory while the validation rule stays INFO/default-on (non-blocking, and `RULE_STATUS` stays `pass`).\n193: It can add traceability context and planning-seed work, but must not alter the iteration final-line contract below unless a later enforcement rollout explicitly changes severity.\n194: \n195: ### Iteration Final-Line Contract (MANDATORY)\n196: \n197: Every `iteration-NNN.md` MUST end with exactly one of the following plain-text lines as the **absolute final line** (no trailing whitespace, no variation), and every review MUST emit exactly one parseable verdict:\n198: \n199: ```\n200: Review verdict: PASS\n201: ```\n202: \n203: ```\n204: Review verdict: CONDITIONAL\n205: ```\n206: \n207: ```\n208: Review verdict: FAIL\n209: ```\n210: \n211: **Mapping rule:** PASS if no P0 or P1 findings in this iteration. CONDITIONAL if any P1 (but no P0) findings. FAIL if any P0 findings. P2-only findings → PASS.\n212: \n213: **VERDICT_LOCK:** Any confirmed active P0 forces the exact final line `Review verdict: FAIL` -- never relabel that state as conditional, partial, mixed, or advisory, and truncated/partial output is not a valid substitute for the final line. An optional advisory `riskScore` may appear in narratives/JSONL for relative risk calibration but never changes the `PASS|CONDITIONAL|FAIL` mapping.\n214: \n215: Downstream automation (including the synthesis phase and CI gate parser) parses this final line via exact string match, do not vary the format.\n216: \n217: **What the check proves:** the post-dispatch verifier validates the *shape* of this line, and nothing more: exactly one well-formed verdict line, and it is final.\n218: It does not read findings, so a `PASS` written above active P1 findings satisfies it, and no parser here makes the line true.\n219: The verdict a release decision reads is recomputed from the findings registry.\n220: In a fan-out run the cross-lineage merge is the authoritative rollup, where any active P0, or any lineage's required quality gate still failing at its stop, becomes a merged FAIL.\n221: Treat the final line as the leaf's self-report and the registry as the record.\n222: \n223: ### Executor Selection Contract\n224: \n225: Executor settings are owned by the YAML workflow and rendered prompt pack -- never hand-dispatch review iterations; each iteration stays LEAF-only and produces the required markdown plus JSONL delta. Full contract (per-iteration invariants, failure modes, JSONL audit field, config surface/precedence, TrustState surface): `references/protocol/loop-protocol.md`.\n226: \n227: ### Stop Decision, Counters and Limits\n228: \n229: `runtime/scripts/convergence.cjs` computes the whole stop decision once per pass from the config, the state log, the findings registry and the coverage graph. `deep-review.yaml` renders no stop arithmetic of its own. The frozen stop reasons are `converged`, `maxIterationsReached`, `blockedStop`, `manualStop`, `error`, `stuckRecovery`, `userPaused` and `maxDurationReached`. A missing graph decision blocks every non-terminal STOP.\n230: \n231: The stuck count and the consecutive failure count are rebuilt from the state log on every pass, so a resumed run counts the same as one that never paused. Failed iterations stay out of the ratio signals. Three trailing failed iterations stop the loop with stop reason `error`. In confirm mode only, the workflow first asks the operator Retry, Skip or Cancel. Auto mode has no such question.\n232: \n233: Each pass also compares `maxDurationMinutes` with the config `createdAt`. When the limit is reached the loop skips the dispatch and goes to synthesis with stop reason `maxDurationReached`.\n234: \n235: ---\n236: \n237: ## 4. RULES\n238: \n239: ### ✅ ALWAYS\n240: \n241: 1. Read JSONL and strategy before review action.\n242: 2. Review one dimension per iteration and write findings to `iteration-NNN.md`.\n243: 3. Append JSONL with severity counts, finding detail, and `newInfoRatio`.\n244: 4. Cite every finding with `[SOURCE: file:line]`. Reject inference-only findings.\n245: 5. Re-read cited code before recording any P0.\n246: 6. Keep target files read-only.\n247: 7. Use `generate-context.js` for continuity saves. **Owner**: the workflow (`deep-review.yaml`) calls it at the save phase, not the reducer (`runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs`) directly — don't expect continuity-save side effects from the reducer alone.\n248: 8. Emit setup `BINDING:` lines before workflow output.\n249: 9. Refuse nested dispatch with: `REFUSE: nested Task tool dispatch is forbidden for LEAF agents. Returning partial findings instead.`\n250: \n251: ### ⛔ NEVER\n252: \n253: 1. **Dispatch sub-agents**, `@deep-review` is LEAF-only. It cannot dispatch additional agents. When dispatch is requested, use the canonical REFUSE wording (ALWAYS rule 9).\n254: 2. **Hold findings in context**, Write everything to iteration files. Context is discarded after each dispatch.\n255: 3. **Exceed TCB**, Stay within `config.maxToolCallsPerIteration` tool calls per iteration. Breadth over depth per cycle.\n256: 4. **Ask the user**, The leaf agent never asks. It makes best-judgment decisions without pausing. The workflow asks only in `:confirm` mode, at its approval gates.\n257: 5. **Skip convergence checks**, Every iteration must be evaluated against convergence criteria before the next dispatch.\n258: 6. **Modify config after init**, review parameters in `deep-review-config.json` are read-only after initialization; the terminal `step_update_config_status` flip to `status: complete` is the one permitted write.\n259: 7. **Modify files under review**, The review loop is observation-only. No code changes during audit.\n260: 8. **Use WebFetch**, Review is code-only. No external resource fetching is permitted.\n261: 9. **Implement fixes during review**, Report findings only. Implementation is a separate follow-up step.\n262: \n263: ### Iteration Status Enum\n264: \n265: `complete | timeout | error | stuck | insight | thought`\n266: \n267: - `insight`: Low newInfoRatio but important finding that changes the verdict trajectory.\n268: \n269: ### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n270: \n271: 1. **3+ consecutive failed iterations**, The loop stops with stop reason `error` and enters synthesis with partial findings. In confirm mode the workflow asks Retry, Skip or Cancel first.\n272: 2. **State file corruption**, Cannot reconstruct iteration history from JSONL or iteration files.\n273: 3. **All dimensions covered with P0 findings remaining**, Human sign-off required before shipping.\n274: 4. **Security vulnerabilities discovered in production code**, Escalate immediately. Do not defer to report synthesis.\n275: 5. **All recovery tiers exhausted**, No automatic recovery path remaining in convergence protocol.\n276: \n277: ---\n278: \n279: ## 5. REFERENCES\n280: \n281: The router discovers reference, asset and script docs dynamically.\n282: Start with these, in order:\n283: \n284: 1. `references/protocol/quick-reference.md`\n285: 2. `references/protocol/loop-protocol.md`\n286: 3. `references/convergence/convergence.md`\n287: 4. `references/convergence/convergence-signals.md`\n288: 5. `references/state/state-format.md`\n289: 6. `references/state/state-outputs.md`\n290: 7. `references/state/state-reducer-registry.md`\n291: 8. `assets/deep-review-dashboard.md`\n292: 9. `assets/deep-review-strategy.md`\n293: \n294: Then load task-specific resources from `references/`, templates from `assets/`, and automation from `scripts/` when present.\n295: \n296: Scripts: `runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs`, `scripts/runtime-capabilities.cjs`.\n297: \n298: Detailed contracts:\n299: \n300: - `references/protocol/loop-protocol.md`: executor invariants, failure modes and config surface.\n301: - `references/protocol/loop-state-and-gates.md`: state transitions, error handling and STOP-decision gates.\n302: - `references/protocol/completion-criteria.md`: the full loop-completion, quality-gate and validation-success checklist.\n303: - `references/state/state-reducer-registry.md`: two-tier content-hash dedup.\n304: \n305: ---\n306: \n307: ## 6. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n308: \n309: A review loop is complete only when convergence and every quality gate agree.\n310: All of these must hold:\n311: \n312: - The composite stop score clears `compositeStopScore`, or `maxIterations` is hit without a false-positive STOP.\n313: - Every configured dimension plus the required traceability protocols (`spec_code`, `checklist_evidence`) has at least one full iteration of coverage.\n314: - All canonical state files exist and parse cleanly: `deep-review-config.json`, `deep-review-state.jsonl`, `deep-review-findings-registry.json`, `deep-review-strategy.md` and `deep-review-dashboard.md` (a fan-out run keeps one per lineage instead), plus one `iterations/iteration-NNN.md` per dispatched iteration.\n315: - `review-report.md` carries all 9 core sections plus the conditional `## Resource Map Coverage Gate`.\n316: - Continuity is saved via `generate-context.js`.\n317: \n318: Nine binary quality gates must all pass before STOP is legal:\n319: \n320: 1. Config validity and lineage match.\n321: 2. Strategy initialization completeness.\n322: 3. State and registry consistency.\n323: 4. Iteration completeness (markdown plus JSONL delta).\n324: 5. Severity-field coverage on every finding (`severity`/`category`/`file:line`/`content_hash`).\n325: 6. The advisory-only `riskScore` never gating verdict logic.\n326: 7. Adversarial P0 replay.\n327: 8. Dimension and protocol coverage stability.\n328: 9. Acceptance coverage: the advisory `AC_COVERAGE` signal when the spec-folder lifecycle predicate is active.\n329: \n330: The security-sensitive override also applies when the target touches security, path handling, env precedence, schema boundaries, persistence or shared policy (`minStabilizationPasses=2` plus fix-completeness replay).\n331: Full gate-by-gate criteria and rationale: `references/protocol/completion-criteria.md`.\n332: \n333: Validate a completed run with `skill_advisor.py \"run a deep review loop\" --threshold 0.8` (skill still surfaces) and `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh <spec-folder> --strict` (exits 0).\n334: \n335: ---\n336: \n337: ## 7. INTEGRATION POINTS\n338: \n339: ### Framework Integration\n340: \n341: This skill operates within the behavioral framework defined in the active runtime's root doc (CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md).\n342: \n343: Key integrations:\n344: - **Gate 2**: Skill routing via `skill_advisor.py` (keywords: deep review, convergence review, iterative review)\n345: - **Gate 3**: File modifications require spec folder question per the root doc Gate 3. The spec folder determines the `{spec_folder}/review/` state packet location\n346: - **Continuity**: `/speckit:resume` is the operator-facing recovery surface, resolving context in the order `handover.md -> _memory.continuity -> spec docs`. During review, the agent writes iteration, strategy, and JSONL state; after synthesis, run `generate-context.js` to write canonical packet continuity\n347: - **Command**: `/deep:review` is the primary invocation point\n348: \n349: ### Code Graph Integration\n350: \n351: The review loop consumes the shared runtime graph and its strict mode-gate validator; packet-specific convergence and state rules remain in this skill.\n352: \n353: ### Related Skills\n354: \n355: Related skills: `deep-research` for investigation loops, `sk-code`'s code-review mode for single-pass review doctrine, and `system-spec-kit` for command-owned state and continuity saves.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/SKILL.md, lines 1-355\n1: ---\n2: name: deep-review\n3: description: \"Autonomous iterative code-review loop with externalized state, convergence detection, P0/P1/P2 findings, fresh context per pass.\"\n4: argument-hint: \"[target] [:auto|:confirm] [--max-iterations=N] [--convergence=N] [--stop-policy=convergence|max-iterations]\"\n5: version: 1.11.17.0\n6: allowed-tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob, Task]\n7: ---\n8: <!-- Note: Task is for the command executor (loop management); @deep-review agent is LEAF-only (no Task). No WebFetch: review is code-only. -->\n9: \n10: # Autonomous Deep Review Loop\n11: \n12: Iterative code review and quality auditing protocol with fresh context per iteration, externalized state, convergence detection, and severity-weighted findings (P0/P1/P2).\n13: \n14: Runtime path resolution:\n15: - OpenCode runtime: `.opencode/agents/*.md`, a link to the `.skilled/agents/*.md` source\n16: - Claude runtime: `.claude/agents/*.md`\n17: - Other runtimes: see `.skilled/bin/agent-mirror-crosswalk.md`\n18: \n19: Convergence threshold semantics and sibling-parity notes (deep-review 0.10 vs deep-research 0.05) live in `references/convergence/convergence.md` §1 under \"Threshold Semantics and Sibling Parity\".\n20: \n21: ## 1. WHEN TO USE\n22: \n23: ### When to Use This Skill\n24: \n25: Use this skill when:\n26: - Code quality audit requiring multiple rounds across different review dimensions\n27: - Spec folder validation requiring cross-reference checks between docs and implementation\n28: - Release readiness check before shipping a feature or component\n29: - Finding misalignments between spec documents and actual code\n30: - Verifying cross-references across documentation, agents, commands, and code\n31: - Iterative review where each dimension's findings inform subsequent dimensions\n32: - Unattended or overnight audit sessions\n33: \n34: ### When NOT to Use\n35: \n36: - Simple single-pass code review (use `sk-code`'s code-review mode instead)\n37: - Known issues that just need fixing (go directly to implementation)\n38: - Implementation tasks (use `sk-code` or `/speckit:implement`)\n39: - Quick one-file checks (use direct Grep/Read)\n40: - Fewer than 2 review dimensions needed (single-pass suffices)\n41: \n42: ### FORBIDDEN INVOCATION PATTERNS\n43: \n44: This skill is invoked EXCLUSIVELY through the `/deep:review` command. The command's YAML workflow owns state, dispatch, and convergence.\n45: \n46: **NEVER:**\n47: - Write a custom bash/shell dispatcher to parallelize iterations (ad-hoc shell fan-out)\n48: - Invoke any CLI executor (cli-codex, cli-claude-code, cli-opencode, cli-cursor, cli-devin, cli-pi, cli-deepseek-harness) directly in a loop to simulate iterations\n49: - Manually write iteration prompts to `/tmp` and dispatch them via `copilot -p`\n50: - Dispatch the `@deep-review` LEAF agent via the Task tool for iteration loops (the agent is LEAF, a single iteration, and MUST be driven by the command's workflow)\n51: - Skip the state machine: `deep-review-state.jsonl`, `deep-review-config.json`, `deltas/`, `prompts/`, `logs/`\n52: - Manage iteration state outside the resolved local review packet under `{spec_folder}/review/`\n53: \n54: **COMMAND-DRIVEN FAN-OUT IS SUPPORTED:** use `--executor`/`--executors`/`--concurrency` flags on `/deep:review`. The command's YAML `step_fanout_spawn` owns multi-lineage dispatch; `fanout-merge.cjs` applies strongest-restriction (any lineage with an active P0, or a required quality gate still failing at its stop → merged FAIL). This is not ad-hoc shell dispatch — it is the canonical fan-out path. Intra-lineage wave orchestration remains deferred.\n55: \n56: **ALWAYS:**\n57: - Invoke via `/deep:review :auto` or `/deep:review :confirm`\n58: - Let the command's YAML workflow own dispatch (auto: `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review.yaml`)\n59: - Record each leaf iteration through the append gateway (`runtime/scripts/append-mode-event.cjs --mode review --run-directory <spec folder> --event-json <file>`) — the gateway is the SINGLE state-log writer: it authorizes, fences, and receipts the write, then refreshes `{state_paths.state_log}` from the ledger\n60: - Treat `runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs` as the reducer for DERIVED artifacts only (finding registry, dashboard metrics, strategy updates); it never writes the state log\n61: - Require every iteration to produce BOTH the markdown narrative AND the JSONL delta (dispatch scripts must fail if either is missing)\n62: - Use `resolveArtifactRoot(specFolder, 'review')` from `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/shared/review-research-paths.cjs` to locate the canonical review root\n63: \n64: ### Trigger Phrases\n65: \n66: - \"review code quality\" / \"audit this code\"\n67: - \"audit spec folder\" / \"validate spec completeness\"\n68: - \"release readiness check\" / \"pre-release review\"\n69: - \"find misalignments\" (between spec and implementation)\n70: - \"verify cross-references\" (across docs and code)\n71: - \"deep review\" / \"iterative review\" / \"review loop\"\n72: - \"quality audit\" / \"convergence detection\"\n73: \n74: ### Keyword Triggers\n75: \n76: `deep review`, `convergence review`, `iterative review`, `review loop`, `release readiness`, `spec folder review`, `convergence detection`, `quality audit`, `find misalignments`, `verify cross-references`, `pre-release review`, `audit spec folder`\n77: \n78: ---\n79: \n80: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n81: \n82: The `system-deep-loop` hub routes an iterative review loop here, and its `review` mode signal selects this packet.\n83: The signal fires when a request names a deep review, a convergence review, a review loop, a release readiness check or a spec-folder audit.\n84: The loop runs only through `/deep:review`, whose workflow owns state, dispatch and convergence.\n85: \n86: `references/protocol/quick-reference.md` loads first on every invocation.\n87: The review phase named by the dispatch context then selects the loop protocol, the convergence documents or the state documents.\n88: \n89: The routing pseudocode in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) is the one route from a request to a resource set.\n90: A request whose intent scores stay under the threshold returns `UNKNOWN_FALLBACK` with a disambiguation checklist, never a silent default.\n91: Read [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n92: \n93: ---\n94: \n95: ## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n96: \n97: ### Architecture\n98: \n99: `/deep:review` owns the loop. The YAML workflow initializes state, dispatches one LEAF review iteration at a time, evaluates convergence, synthesizes `review-report.md`, and saves continuity. The LEAF agent reads state, reviews one dimension, writes `iteration-NNN.md`, updates strategy, and appends JSONL. Full 4-phase lifecycle: `references/protocol/loop-protocol.md`.\n100: \n101: ### Resource Map Coverage Gate\n102: \n103: When `{spec_folder}/resource-map.md` exists at init, deep review treats it as a mandatory audit input and does four things:\n104: \n105: - Persists `resource_map_present` in config.\n106: - Seeds `Known Context` with a snapshot of the map.\n107: - Has at least one iteration audit `target_files` coverage against the map: touched, not-touched with `expected-by-scope`-versus-`gap`, and absent-from-map, with findings tagged `resource-map-coverage`.\n108: - Has synthesis add the `## Resource Map Coverage Gate` report section and a converged `resource-map.md`. Disable this with `--no-resource-map`.\n109: \n110: Absent at init, the gate and section are skipped without failing the loop.\n111: Full sequencing: `references/protocol/loop-protocol.md` (init 9a, iteration 3b, synthesis 4). Schema: `references/state/state-format.md`.\n112: \n113: ### Bounded Context Snapshot Replacement\n114: \n115: During initialization, capture a bounded, pointer-based context snapshot in `deep-review-strategy.md` `Known Context` before the first dimension runs.\n116: The snapshot holds:\n117: \n118: - Target pointers: files, specs, symbols and resource-map entries.\n119: - The claimed behavior and acceptance criteria to verify.\n120: - Reuse and convention pointers.\n121: - Risk areas, missing context, stale-graph caveats and out-of-scope areas.\n122: \n123: Do not create a context-loop report or widen scope beyond the declared target.\n124: Use `@context` for quick retrieval, and `/speckit:plan` only when findings require implementation planning.\n125: \n126: ### State Packet Location\n127: \n128: The review state packet always lives under the target spec's local `review/` folder.\n129: Root-spec targets use `{spec_folder}/review/` directly.\n130: Child-phase and sub-phase targets use **flat-first** placement: the first run writes flat, and a `pt-NN` sibling packet is allocated only when prior content already exists for a different target.\n131: Full resolution rule and worked examples: `references/state/state-format.md` §1.\n132: \n133: Core artifacts: `deep-review-config.json`, `deep-review-state.jsonl`, `deep-review-strategy.md`, `deep-review-dashboard.md`, `.deep-review-pause`, `resource-map.md`, `review-report.md`, and `iterations/iteration-NNN.md`.\n134: \n135: The loop writes `review/review-report.md` itself at synthesis rather than scaffolding it through `create.sh --level review`.\n136: The report is assembled from the findings registry and the JSONL state, so its content exists only once the loop has run, and a scaffolded skeleton would be overwritten whole.\n137: `create.sh --level review` exists for a review packet authored by hand, and its template carries the same nine sections the loop emits.\n138: \n139: ### Core Innovation: Fresh Context Per Iteration\n140: \n141: Each agent dispatch gets a fresh context window; state continuity comes from files, not memory, preventing accumulated findings from biasing later dimensions. Init writes config/strategy/JSONL; each loop reads state, checks convergence, dispatches one dimension, records findings, and reduces state; synthesis compiles the report and saves continuity.\n142: \n143: ### Review Dimensions\n144: \n145: The four primary review dimensions (configured in `assets/review-mode-contract.yaml`):\n146: \n147: | Dimension | Focus | Key Questions |\n148: |-----------|-------|---------------|\n149: | **Correctness** | Logic, behavior, error handling | Behavior matches claims? Edge cases handled? |\n150: | **Security** | Vulnerabilities, exposure, trust boundaries | Inputs validated? Credentials exposed? |\n151: | **Spec-Alignment / Traceability** | Spec vs. implementation fidelity | Code matches spec.md? Planned items present? |\n152: | **Completeness / Maintainability** | Coverage, dead code, documentation | TODOs resolved? Code self-documenting? |\n153: \n154: ### Lifecycle + Reducer Contract\n155: \n156: Review mode is lineage-aware.\n157: \n158: - **Lifecycle modes:** `new`, `resume`, and `restart`.\n159: - **Required lineage fields:** `sessionId`, `parentSessionId`, `lineageMode`, `generation`, `continuedFromRun`, and `releaseReadinessState`.\n160: - **Reducer contract:** consumes the latest JSONL delta, the new iteration file, and prior reduced state, then emits finding registry, dashboard metrics, and strategy updates.\n161: \n162: ### Severity Classification\n163: \n164: | Severity | Criteria | Blocking |\n165: |----------|----------|---------|\n166: | **P0** | Correctness failure, security vulnerability, spec contradiction | Yes, blocks PASS verdict |\n167: | **P1** | Degraded behavior, incomplete implementation, missing validation | Conditional, triggers CONDITIONAL verdict |\n168: | **P2** | Style, naming, minor improvements, documentation gaps | No, PASS with advisories |\n169: \n170: P0 and P1 also follow the reach calibration in `sk-code-review`'s `references/review-core.md` §2.\n171: A blocking finding names an input or call path the code's stated contract allows, or a real caller that sends the failing input.\n172: \"Missing validation\" is P1 only when the unvalidated input is in reach. Validation the code never claimed, for input outside its stated contract, is P2 hardening.\n173: At a trust boundary every value the outside can send is in reach.\n174: \n175: The scale has exactly three tiers, and no fourth is reserved.\n176: A severity outside P0/P1/P2 is not a fourth tier. That covers a `P3` inherited from an older four-level convention, and any unlisted word.\n177: Collapse it to the tier its impact actually matches (a `P3` becomes `P2`) and keep the original rating visible in the finding, so the rating is converted rather than lost.\n178: An unconverted out-of-scale severity reaches the cross-lineage merge unranked, where it sorts below every P2 and cannot raise the merged verdict.\n179: \n180: ### Verdicts\n181: \n182: | Verdict | Condition |\n183: |---------|-----------|\n184: | **PASS** | No P0/P1 findings and no open search debt, P2 findings recorded as advisories (`hasAdvisories: true`) |\n185: | **CONDITIONAL** | P1 findings present, or open search debt remains, with remediation plan included in report |\n186: | **FAIL** | Any P0 finding confirmed after adversarial self-check, or a required quality gate (evidence, scope, coverage) still failing at the terminal stop |\n187: \n188: ### Acceptance-Coverage Signal\n189: \n190: When the review target is a spec folder, deep review reflects the `AC_COVERAGE` validation signal in synthesis for Level 2+ folders.\n191: The signal applies only once `checklist.md` exists and `implementation-summary.md` is in-progress or later. Level 1 folders and fresh scaffolds are exempt.\n192: It is advisory while the validation rule stays INFO/default-on (non-blocking, and `RULE_STATUS` stays `pass`).\n193: It can add traceability context and planning-seed work, but must not alter the iteration final-line contract below unless a later enforcement rollout explicitly changes severity.\n194: \n195: ### Iteration Final-Line Contract (MANDATORY)\n196: \n197: Every `iteration-NNN.md` MUST end with exactly one of the following plain-text lines as the **absolute final line** (no trailing whitespace, no variation), and every review MUST emit exactly one parseable verdict:\n198: \n199: ```\n200: Review verdict: PASS\n201: ```\n202: \n203: ```\n204: Review verdict: CONDITIONAL\n205: ```\n206: \n207: ```\n208: Review verdict: FAIL\n209: ```\n210: \n211: **Mapping rule:** PASS if no P0 or P1 findings in this iteration. CONDITIONAL if any P1 (but no P0) findings. FAIL if any P0 findings. P2-only findings → PASS.\n212: \n213: **VERDICT_LOCK:** Any confirmed active P0 forces the exact final line `Review verdict: FAIL` -- never relabel that state as conditional, partial, mixed, or advisory, and truncated/partial output is not a valid substitute for the final line. An optional advisory `riskScore` may appear in narratives/JSONL for relative risk calibration but never changes the `PASS|CONDITIONAL|FAIL` mapping.\n214: \n215: Downstream automation (including the synthesis phase and CI gate parser) parses this final line via exact string match, do not vary the format.\n216: \n217: **What the check proves:** the post-dispatch verifier validates the *shape* of this line, and nothing more: exactly one well-formed verdict line, and it is final.\n218: It does not read findings, so a `PASS` written above active P1 findings satisfies it, and no parser here makes the line true.\n219: The verdict a release decision reads is recomputed from the findings registry.\n220: In a fan-out run the cross-lineage merge is the authoritative rollup, where any active P0, or any lineage's required quality gate still failing at its stop, becomes a merged FAIL.\n221: Treat the final line as the leaf's self-report and the registry as the record.\n222: \n223: ### Executor Selection Contract\n224: \n225: Executor settings are owned by the YAML workflow and rendered prompt pack -- never hand-dispatch review iterations; each iteration stays LEAF-only and produces the required markdown plus JSONL delta. Full contract (per-iteration invariants, failure modes, JSONL audit field, config surface/precedence, TrustState surface): `references/protocol/loop-protocol.md`.\n226: \n227: ### Stop Decision, Counters and Limits\n228: \n229: `runtime/scripts/convergence.cjs` computes the whole stop decision once per pass from the config, the state log, the findings registry and the coverage graph. `deep-review.yaml` renders no stop arithmetic of its own. The frozen stop reasons are `converged`, `maxIterationsReached`, `blockedStop`, `manualStop`, `error`, `stuckRecovery`, `userPaused` and `maxDurationReached`. A missing graph decision blocks every non-terminal STOP.\n230: \n231: The stuck count and the consecutive failure count are rebuilt from the state log on every pass, so a resumed run counts the same as one that never paused. Failed iterations stay out of the ratio signals. Three trailing failed iterations stop the loop with stop reason `error`. In confirm mode only, the workflow first asks the operator Retry, Skip or Cancel. Auto mode has no such question.\n232: \n233: Each pass also compares `maxDurationMinutes` with the config `createdAt`. When the limit is reached the loop skips the dispatch and goes to synthesis with stop reason `maxDurationReached`.\n234: \n235: ---\n236: \n237: ## 4. RULES\n238: \n239: ### ✅ ALWAYS\n240: \n241: 1. Read JSONL and strategy before review action.\n242: 2. Review one dimension per iteration and write findings to `iteration-NNN.md`.\n243: 3. Append JSONL with severity counts, finding detail, and `newInfoRatio`.\n244: 4. Cite every finding with `[SOURCE: file:line]`. Reject inference-only findings.\n245: 5. Re-read cited code before recording any P0.\n246: 6. Keep target files read-only.\n247: 7. Use `generate-context.js` for continuity saves. **Owner**: the workflow (`deep-review.yaml`) calls it at the save phase, not the reducer (`runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs`) directly — don't expect continuity-save side effects from the reducer alone.\n248: 8. Emit setup `BINDING:` lines before workflow output.\n249: 9. Refuse nested dispatch with: `REFUSE: nested Task tool dispatch is forbidden for LEAF agents. Returning partial findings instead.`\n250: \n251: ### ⛔ NEVER\n252: \n253: 1. **Dispatch sub-agents**, `@deep-review` is LEAF-only. It cannot dispatch additional agents. When dispatch is requested, use the canonical REFUSE wording (ALWAYS rule 9).\n254: 2. **Hold findings in context**, Write everything to iteration files. Context is discarded after each dispatch.\n255: 3. **Exceed TCB**, Stay within `config.maxToolCallsPerIteration` tool calls per iteration. Breadth over depth per cycle.\n256: 4. **Ask the user**, The leaf agent never asks. It makes best-judgment decisions without pausing. The workflow asks only in `:confirm` mode, at its approval gates.\n257: 5. **Skip convergence checks**, Every iteration must be evaluated against convergence criteria before the next dispatch.\n258: 6. **Modify config after init**, review parameters in `deep-review-config.json` are read-only after initialization; the terminal `step_update_config_status` flip to `status: complete` is the one permitted write.\n259: 7. **Modify files under review**, The review loop is observation-only. No code changes during audit.\n260: 8. **Use WebFetch**, Review is code-only. No external resource fetching is permitted.\n261: 9. **Implement fixes during review**, Report findings only. Implementation is a separate follow-up step.\n262: \n263: ### Iteration Status Enum\n264: \n265: `complete | timeout | error | stuck | insight | thought`\n266: \n267: - `insight`: Low newInfoRatio but important finding that changes the verdict trajectory.\n268: \n269: ### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n270: \n271: 1. **3+ consecutive failed iterations**, The loop stops with stop reason `error` and enters synthesis with partial findings. In confirm mode the workflow asks Retry, Skip or Cancel first.\n272: 2. **State file corruption**, Cannot reconstruct iteration history from JSONL or iteration files.\n273: 3. **All dimensions covered with P0 findings remaining**, Human sign-off required before shipping.\n274: 4. **Security vulnerabilities discovered in production code**, Escalate immediately. Do not defer to report synthesis.\n275: 5. **All recovery tiers exhausted**, No automatic recovery path remaining in convergence protocol.\n276: \n277: ---\n278: \n279: ## 5. REFERENCES\n280: \n281: The router discovers reference, asset and script docs dynamically.\n282: Start with these, in order:\n283: \n284: 1. `references/protocol/quick-reference.md`\n285: 2. `references/protocol/loop-protocol.md`\n286: 3. `references/convergence/convergence.md`\n287: 4. `references/convergence/convergence-signals.md`\n288: 5. `references/state/state-format.md`\n289: 6. `references/state/state-outputs.md`\n290: 7. `references/state/state-reducer-registry.md`\n291: 8. `assets/deep-review-dashboard.md`\n292: 9. `assets/deep-review-strategy.md`\n293: \n294: Then load task-specific resources from `references/`, templates from `assets/`, and automation from `scripts/` when present.\n295: \n296: Scripts: `runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs`, `scripts/runtime-capabilities.cjs`.\n297: \n298: Detailed contracts:\n299: \n300: - `references/protocol/loop-protocol.md`: executor invariants, failure modes and config surface.\n301: - `references/protocol/loop-state-and-gates.md`: state transitions, error handling and STOP-decision gates.\n302: - `references/protocol/completion-criteria.md`: the full loop-completion, quality-gate and validation-success checklist.\n303: - `references/state/state-reducer-registry.md`: two-tier content-hash dedup.\n304: \n305: ---\n306: \n307: ## 6. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n308: \n309: A review loop is complete only when convergence and every quality gate agree.\n310: All of these must hold:\n311: \n312: - The composite stop score clears `compositeStopScore`, or `maxIterations` is hit without a false-positive STOP.\n313: - Every configured dimension plus the required traceability protocols (`spec_code`, `checklist_evidence`) has at least one full iteration of coverage.\n314: - All canonical state files exist and parse cleanly: `deep-review-config.json`, `deep-review-state.jsonl`, `deep-review-findings-registry.json`, `deep-review-strategy.md` and `deep-review-dashboard.md` (a fan-out run keeps one per lineage instead), plus one `iterations/iteration-NNN.md` per dispatched iteration.\n315: - `review-report.md` carries all 9 core sections plus the conditional `## Resource Map Coverage Gate`.\n316: - Continuity is saved via `generate-context.js`.\n317: \n318: Nine binary quality gates must all pass before STOP is legal:\n319: \n320: 1. Config validity and lineage match.\n321: 2. Strategy initialization completeness.\n322: 3. State and registry consistency.\n323: 4. Iteration completeness (markdown plus JSONL delta).\n324: 5. Severity-field coverage on every finding (`severity`/`category`/`file:line`/`content_hash`).\n325: 6. The advisory-only `riskScore` never gating verdict logic.\n326: 7. Adversarial P0 replay.\n327: 8. Dimension and protocol coverage stability.\n328: 9. Acceptance coverage: the advisory `AC_COVERAGE` signal when the spec-folder lifecycle predicate is active.\n329: \n330: The security-sensitive override also applies when the target touches security, path handling, env precedence, schema boundaries, persistence or shared policy (`minStabilizationPasses=2` plus fix-completeness replay).\n331: Full gate-by-gate criteria and rationale: `references/protocol/completion-criteria.md`.\n332: \n333: Validate a completed run with `skill_advisor.py \"run a deep review loop\" --threshold 0.8` (skill still surfaces) and `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh <spec-folder> --strict` (exits 0).\n334: \n335: ---\n336: \n337: ## 7. INTEGRATION POINTS\n338: \n339: ### Framework Integration\n340: \n341: This skill operates within the behavioral framework defined in the active runtime's root doc (CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md).\n342: \n343: Key integrations:\n344: - **Gate 2**: Skill routing via `skill_advisor.py` (keywords: deep review, convergence review, iterative review)\n345: - **Gate 3**: File modifications require spec folder question per the root doc Gate 3. The spec folder determines the `{spec_folder}/review/` state packet location\n346: - **Continuity**: `/speckit:resume` is the operator-facing recovery surface, resolving context in the order `handover.md -> _memory.continuity -> spec docs`. During review, the agent writes iteration, strategy, and JSONL state; after synthesis, run `generate-context.js` to write canonical packet continuity\n347: - **Command**: `/deep:review` is the primary invocation point\n348: \n349: ### Code Graph Integration\n350: \n351: The review loop consumes the shared runtime graph and its strict mode-gate validator; packet-specific convergence and state rules remain in this skill.\n352: \n353: ### Related Skills\n354: \n355: Related skills: `deep-research` for investigation loops, `sk-code`'s code-review mode for single-pass review doctrine, and `system-spec-kit` for command-owned state and continuity saves."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/SKILL.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794847600412,"end":1794847601301}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794847601306,"sessionID":"ses_e1abf3e72ffecbrpVf6MWZonWP","part":{"partID":"prt_1e540f0db0012j7Lm67jNqda5i","sessionID":"ses_e1abf3e72ffecbrpVf6MWZonWP","messageID":"msg_1e540c5d3001xEGsTW9qm6NyRC","type":"tool","id":"call_6e1f3ef1c92243f8b774c954","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/review-brief-10.txt"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/review-brief-10.txt, lines 1-10\n1: Code review pass ten of command surfacing. Scope: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/review-scope-10.txt lists files as area TAB path. Your lineage label is the last folder of config.fanout_lineage_artifact_dir: attach-scorer-10 reviews area attach-scorer, docs-merge-10 area docs-merge. Read each diff with the command on the scope file's first line, then the whole file. attach-scorer covers the mention check that now drops backticks, asterisks and double quotes before it reads a refusal or question lead (commandOnlyMentioned), the shared scratch-folder pattern in the transcript scorer (insideCheckout's no-checkout fallback and guardedWriteCounts), the re-captured routing baseline where two slash-variant rows moved from offered to withheld, and the bench's Vibe row. docs-merge covers whether each doc claim matches the code at HEAD, and whether each merge resolution kept main's change and the branch's /create:sk-skill name without dropping either side's content.\n2: \n3: Cover all four dimensions (correctness, security, traceability, maintainability), and review every scope file of your area under at least two of them.\n4: \n5: Run each iteration as the deep-review workflow defines it, including its graph and stop steps, which earlier passes skipped:\n6: 1. Write the iteration record to your state log with dimensions, filesReviewed, findingsNew, newFindingsRatio and a graphEvents array: one dimension node per dimension you covered, one file node per file you read, one finding node per finding, one evidence node per finding naming its file:line, and edges COVERS (dimension to file), IN_DIMENSION and IN_FILE (finding to dimension and file) and EVIDENCE_FOR (evidence to finding). The shape is in .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/state/state-jsonl.md under Graph Events.\n7: 2. Persist them: node .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/upsert.cjs --spec-folder specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build --loop-type review --session-id <your config sessionId> --nodes '<json array>' --edges '<json array>', with kind and relation upper case.\n8: 3. Decide whether to stop: node .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/convergence.cjs --spec-folder specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build --loop-type review --session-id <your config sessionId> --convergence-mode default --stop-decision --config-path <absolute path of your deep-review-config.json> --state-log <absolute path of your deep-review-state.jsonl> --registry-path <absolute path of your deep-review-findings-registry.json> --iteration-count <iterations done> --security-sensitive-fix-scope false --fix-replay-required-rows 0 --fix-replay-passing-rows 0. Record its decision, stopReason and blockedBy in an event line of your state log. When the decision is STOP, synthesize and end with that stopReason; otherwise continue, aiming the next iteration at what blockedBy or the graph trace names.\n9: \n10: Do not re-report these recorded limits unless you show a new input that breaks them: a suffix followed by a file extension such as :auto.md reads as the command, as it did before this branch; a node -e arrow inside escaped quotes still reads as a redirect; isolation-check.sh is macOS only. Underscore emphasis around a command name is not dropped, because a command name may hold an underscore. The two moved baseline rows are judged without their text: 038-alignment-p1-remediation/scratch/lead-diag.mjs prints only lead indices. The open P2 list in 038-alignment-p1-remediation/scratch/alignment-final-dispositions.md is already recorded. Every finding is P0, P1 or P2 with file:line and a concrete input that shows it. HOLDOUT RULE: never open command-prompts.jsonl, holdout-prompts.jsonl or behaviour-fresh-prompts.jsonl except through jq 'del(.prompt)', never run git diff, show or log -p over a path that includes them without the pathspecs ':(exclude)**/command-prompts.jsonl' ':(exclude)**/holdout-prompts.jsonl' ':(exclude)**/behaviour-fresh-prompts.jsonl', never run score-command-transcripts.mjs --summary, and never print session text from a CLI session store. Never read or create .skilled/hooks/hook-flags.env. The reviewer changes no code; it writes only inside its lineage folder and through the two scripts above.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/review-brief-10.txt, lines 1-10\n1: Code review pass ten of command surfacing. Scope: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/review-scope-10.txt lists files as area TAB path. Your lineage label is the last folder of config.fanout_lineage_artifact_dir: attach-scorer-10 reviews area attach-scorer, docs-merge-10 area docs-merge. Read each diff with the command on the scope file's first line, then the whole file. attach-scorer covers the mention check that now drops backticks, asterisks and double quotes before it reads a refusal or question lead (commandOnlyMentioned), the shared scratch-folder pattern in the transcript scorer (insideCheckout's no-checkout fallback and guardedWriteCounts), the re-captured routing baseline where two slash-variant rows moved from offered to withheld, and the bench's Vibe row. docs-merge covers whether each doc claim matches the code at HEAD, and whether each merge resolution kept main's change and the branch's /create:sk-skill name without dropping either side's content.\n2: \n3: Cover all four dimensions (correctness, security, traceability, maintainability), and review every scope file of your area under at least two of them.\n4: \n5: Run each iteration as the deep-review workflow defines it, including its graph and stop steps, which earlier passes skipped:\n6: 1. Write the iteration record to your state log with dimensions, filesReviewed, findingsNew, newFindingsRatio and a graphEvents array: one dimension node per dimension you covered, one file node per file you read, one finding node per finding, one evidence node per finding naming its file:line, and edges COVERS (dimension to file), IN_DIMENSION and IN_FILE (finding to dimension and file) and EVIDENCE_FOR (evidence to finding). The shape is in .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/state/state-jsonl.md under Graph Events.\n7: 2. Persist them: node .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/upsert.cjs --spec-folder specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build --loop-type review --session-id <your config sessionId> --nodes '<json array>' --edges '<json array>', with kind and relation upper case.\n8: 3. Decide whether to stop: node .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/convergence.cjs --spec-folder specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build --loop-type review --session-id <your config sessionId> --convergence-mode default --stop-decision --config-path <absolute path of your deep-review-config.json> --state-log <absolute path of your deep-review-state.jsonl> --registry-path <absolute path of your deep-review-findings-registry.json> --iteration-count <iterations done> --security-sensitive-fix-scope false --fix-replay-required-rows 0 --fix-replay-passing-rows 0. Record its decision, stopReason and blockedBy in an event line of your state log. When the decision is STOP, synthesize and end with that stopReason; otherwise continue, aiming the next iteration at what blockedBy or the graph trace names.\n9: \n10: Do not re-report these recorded limits unless you show a new input that breaks them: a suffix followed by a file extension such as :auto.md reads as the command, as it did before this branch; a node -e arrow inside escaped quotes still reads as a redirect; isolation-check.sh is macOS only. Underscore emphasis around a command name is not dropped, because a command name may hold an underscore. The two moved baseline rows are judged without their text: 038-alignment-p1-remediation/scratch/lead-diag.mjs prints only lead indices. The open P2 list in 038-alignment-p1-remediation/scratch/alignment-final-dispositions.md is already recorded. Every finding is P0, P1 or P2 with file:line and a concrete input that shows it. HOLDOUT RULE: never open command-prompts.jsonl, holdout-prompts.jsonl or behaviour-fresh-prompts.jsonl except through jq 'del(.prompt)', never run git diff, show or log -p over a path that includes them without the pathspecs ':(exclude)**/command-prompts.jsonl' ':(exclude)**/holdout-prompts.jsonl' ':(exclude)**/behaviour-fresh-prompts.jsonl', never run score-command-transcripts.mjs --summary, and never print session text from a CLI session store. Never read or create .skilled/hooks/hook-flags.env. The reviewer changes no code; it writes only inside its lineage folder and through the two scripts above."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/review-brief-10.txt\"}"},"time":{"start":1794847600859,"end":1794847601306}}}}
